  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Issued in two gatherings of 36 pages; printed on newspaper. Sewn spine. Typeset in Amasis and Apercu.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The second edition "was printed in an edition of 150 and distributed in October, 2015 on the occasion of the exhibition entitled 'Writing-in-threads' which occured at 99 [cent symbol] Plus Gallery in Brooklyn, NY. This edition was released with a group reading at Printed Matter in Manhattan, NY"--Colophon.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">"The first edition of this newspaper was printed in an edition of 100 for the for the occasion of an exhibition of the same name that took place at Brown University's Granoff Center in May 2015"--Colophon.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">"Gratitude to the Anni and Josef Albers Foundation and the Creative Arts Council at Brown University for their generous support"--Colophon.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Includes "Index of draft notations."</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Afterword by Francesca Capone.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="520" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">A compilation of images of woven cloth, printed e-mail "threads" and poems that examines the poetics of weaving traditions, focusing on fiber-based forms as a medium for record keeping, storytelling and poetry. Fifteen poets and writers were invited to respond to the artist's weavings, sent as attachments to e-mail messages, making "Writing in threads" a type of communication between an online stitching circle and a community of literary writers.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="520" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">"Writing in threads takes on meaning beyond metaphor. Weaving is recognized as an alternative analog equivalent to digital writing forms. As paperless culture becomes ubiquitous, the thread has taken hold in numerous platforms. Gmail organizes e-mails into threads, and so do most cell phone text messaging softwares. We compile threads of instant messages, twitter feeds, Facebook messages, and so on."--Pages 82-83.</subfield>
  </datafield>
